>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Procurement · Quality Control · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>Quality Control and Shipment Inspection for Battery Imports</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Quality in a battery import is not discovered at the destination — it is decided at three gates:
        what goes into the product, what leaves the factory, and what arrives. An importer who skips the
        middle gate (pre-shipment inspection) has no lever between production and payment.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Build a three-gate QC chain — <strong>first-article inspection</strong> before mass
        production, <strong>pre-shipment inspection (PSI)</strong> on a statistically-defined sample before release, and
        <strong>arrival testing</strong> as the final audit — and fix the sampling rule in the contract. The method
        complements the <a href="/procurement/battery-factory-evaluation-checklist/">factory evaluation checklist</a>, which
        audits capability; this guide audits the goods.
      </div>

      <h2>Gate 1: First-article inspection</h2>
      <p>
        The first article is the first production unit — or a pre-production sample — built to the agreed
        specification, and its inspection is the cheapest mistake you will ever catch. Before mass
        production, verify on the actual unit:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Construction.</strong> Plate count and geometry, separator quality, fill and seal — the invisible internals behind the <a href="/technology/how-lead-acid-battery-works/">chemistry's performance</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Dimensions and terminals.</strong> Group-size fitment and <a href="/lead-acid/battery-terminal-types/">terminal type</a> against the drawing, not the brochure.</li>
        <li><strong>Ratings under the stated standard.</strong> CCA and Ah tested under the method the datasheet names — see <a href="/procurement/verify-cca-claims-bulk-purchase/">verifying CCA claims</a> for the standard trap.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight.</strong> Plate material differences are hard to hide from a scale; compare against the <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/weight-chart/">weight data</a> on the data sub-site.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Changes at first-article stage cost a conversation; the same changes after a container is built cost
        a claim.
      </p>

      <h2>Gate 2: Pre-shipment inspection (PSI)</h2>
      <p>
        PSI is the third-party check at the factory or warehouse before release for shipment. Its power comes
        from the <strong>sampling rule</strong>: how many units are opened, measured and tested, and how many defects
        are tolerable. The reproducible way to set the rule is AQL sampling per
        <strong>ISO 2859-1</strong>, which ties a lot size and an AQL level to a sample size and accept/reject defect
        counts. Defects are graded:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Critical</strong> — safety or non-conformance that fails the product: cracked cases, reversed polarity, leaking acid, zero voltage. Even one critical defect typically rejects the lot.</li>
        <li><strong>Major</strong> — functional or commercial failure: CCA or capacity below spec, wrong terminals, damaged covers, missing labels.</li>
        <li><strong>Minor</strong> — cosmetic: scratches, smeared printing, uneven strap welds with no functional impact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A useful PSI report is not a certificate — it is a dataset: sample size and plan, the unit serial or
        date codes checked, the measurements taken (voltage, weight, dimensions), the test method and
        equipment for any performance spot-check, photos of defects, and an accept/rework/reject
        recommendation. A report without the sampling plan is a summary of feelings.
      </p>

      <h2>Gate 3: Arrival testing</h2>
      <p>
        Arrival testing is the audit that catches what happened between the factory gate and your warehouse —
        and what PSI could not see. The three checks with the highest catch rate:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Open-circuit voltage per unit.</strong> A fully-charged 12V lead-acid battery should read at its charged resting voltage; a low reading flags a discharged, shorted or aged unit. See the <a href="/lead-acid/12v-voltage-chart/">12V voltage chart</a> for the reference values.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight against the datasheet.</strong> The cheapest independent check of plate material.</li>
        <li><strong>Performance spot-check.</strong> CCA via a calibrated conductance or discharge tester, or capacity under the standard discharge rate, on a sample drawn to the AQL plan — the same logic as <a href="/testing/battery-capacity-testing/">capacity testing</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Date codes.</strong> Verify freshness — batteries age in storage, and <a href="/lead-acid/battery-date-code/">reading the date code</a> tells you how long the lot sat.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Writing QC into the contract</h2>
      <p>
        All three gates are worth little if they are not contractual. The purchase order should name the
        specification version, the AQL levels for critical/major/minor defects, who performs PSI and who pays,
        the test method and standard for performance spot-checks, and the remedy when a gate fails —
        rework, discount, replacement or rejection. Agreeing the remedy in advance turns a failed inspection
        into a defined process instead of a negotiation under time pressure. The same discipline applies to
        the <a href="/procurement/moq-payment-incoterms-battery-imports/">commercial terms</a> around the inspection.
      </p>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is a first-article inspection required before mass production — and does it cover construction, terminals, ratings and weight?</li>
        <li>Does the PSI report state the sampling plan (lot size, AQL level, sample size) and the test method, not just a pass/fail verdict?</li>
        <li>Are critical, major and minor defects judged against different limits, with the lot rejected on criticals?</li>
        <li>Does the contract name the AQL levels, who performs and pays for PSI, and the remedy for a failed gate?</li>
        <li>Is arrival testing planned — voltage, weight, performance spot-check and date codes — on the same sampling discipline?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is pre-shipment inspection (PSI) for batteries?</h3>
      <p>Pre-shipment inspection is a third-party check performed at the factory or warehouse before the goods are released for shipment. For batteries it typically covers quantity and packing, dimensions, terminals and polarity, open-circuit voltage, weight, labelling and date codes, and performance spot-checks such as CCA or capacity on a sample. The output is a report the buyer uses to accept, rework or reject the lot.</p>
      <h3>What is AQL sampling and how does it apply to battery inspection?</h3>
      <p>AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit) sampling, defined in ISO 2859-1, is a statistical scheme that decides how many units to inspect and how many defects are tolerable, given a lot size and an AQL level. It converts "check a random sample" into a reproducible rule — a given lot size and AQL level dictates a sample size and accept/reject defect counts. Critical, major and minor defects are judged against different limits.</p>
      <h3>Which tests belong in a battery arrival inspection?</h3>
      <p>The checks that catch most problems: open-circuit voltage per unit (a low reading flags a discharged, shorted or aged unit), weight versus the datasheet (plate material differences show up as weight), and a performance spot-check — CCA via a conductance or discharge tester, or capacity under the standard discharge rate — on a sample drawn to the AQL plan. Date codes should also be verified against freshness.</p>
      <h3>What is a first-article inspection and why does it matter for batteries?</h3>
      <p>First-article inspection (FAI) examines and tests the first production units — or a pre-production sample — against the agreed specification before mass production runs. For batteries it is the moment design intent meets physical reality: plate construction, fill weights, terminal geometry and ratings are verified on actual units, and changes are cheap to make before thousands of units exist.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Synthesized from inspection and sampling standards plus battery trade practice; this guide names no
        inspection companies and quotes no defect-rate statistics — the sampling plan is given as a method
        the buyer applies to their own lots.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>ISO 2859-1 — sampling procedures for inspection by attributes (AQL schemes).</li>
        <li>SAE J537 and EN 50342 — the cranking and capacity test methods referenced by performance spot-checks.</li>
        <li>Battery Council International — group size, terminal and rating definitions used in inspection criteria.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub testing sub-site — <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/">standard test conditions</a> for the methods behind spot-checks.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub data sub-site — <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/">battery group data</a> and <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/weight-chart/">weight reference</a> used as inspection targets.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub procurement cluster — <a href="/procurement/battery-factory-evaluation-checklist/">factory evaluation checklist</a> and <a href="/procurement/verify-cca-claims-bulk-purchase/">CCA claim verification</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-22</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
